  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Space Exploration Technologies has $2.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Space Exploration Technologies won 80 awards so far, with an average award size of $35.6M, and obligations are down 5.0% year over year. Obligations rose from $1.5B in FY21 to $2.1B in FY22 and $2.4B in FY23, reached $3.4B in FY24, then recorded $3B in FY25 and $2.8B in FY26. The Department of Defense leads buying with $2.2B in obligations, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $684M. Major markets include Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $1.6B, Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $519M, and Satellite Telecommunications at $474M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounts for $250M, and Engineering Services shows $765K. The figures reflect contract obligations through the fiscal year to date and include award counts and average award size as reported for FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers of Space Exploration Technologies?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, with $2.2B in obligations. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration follows with $684M. Other named buyers in the top list include the Department of State at $3.2M, the Department of Homeland Security at $1.5M, the Department of Justice at $600K, and the Department of Transportation at $9K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which markets generate the most federal revenue for Space Exploration Technologies?",
        "body": "Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation is the largest market at $1.6B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing contributes $519M and Satellite Telecommunications contributes $474M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounts for $250M and Engineering Services shows $765K."
      }
    ]
  },
